{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Clapham1969-rhomb-b-NdF3-MgF2",
  "Source": "P. B. Clapham, M. J. Downs, and R. J. King, \"Some applications of thin films to polarization devices,\" Appl. Opt. 8, 1965-1974 (1969). doi:10.1364/AO.8.001965 - Table III footnote b (p. 1970); text p. 1971",
  "Notes": "Achromatic Fresnel-rhomb quarter-wave retarder in fused silica: each of the two total-internal-reflection faces carries 120 A NdF3 + 340 A MgF2 (symmetrical arrangement, Table III column b)\nSpecification: Total phase retardation of the rhomb (two identical reflections) = 90 deg from 214 to 546 nm.\nReported: Table III column b (computed), total retardation for TWO identical reflections at 54 deg, i.e. 2 x this surface: 86.2, 87.7, 90.1, 90.8, 90.4, 90.5 deg at 546, 405, 334, 289, 248, 214 nm (per surface 43.10, 43.85, 45.05, 45.40, 45.20, 45.25 deg). Uncoated rhomb at 54 deg (column a, two reflections): 84.55, 85.8, 87.05, 88.45, 90.4, 93.15 deg at the same wavelengths.\nNotes: Reflection is total internal reflection from inside the silica; incident medium = silica, exit medium = air beyond the films, angle in the silica. Phase retardation delta = difference of the p and s reflection phase changes (Eq. (4) for the bare surface). Film indices: computations used MgF2 1.385 at 546 nm rising to 1.419 at 214 nm and NdF3 1.605 at 546 nm rising to 1.70 at 214 nm (p. 1971); only these end points are printed, so a two-term Cauchy through them is used (intermediate values are an assumption). Thicknesses in angstrom in the paper, converted to nm. Films are NdF3 (high index) next to the silica, MgF2 on top (outer, air side): the text (p. 1971) says the high-index film raises delta and the further addition of a low-index film depresses it. A check with n_silica = 1.46 at 546 nm and this order reproduces Table III (opposite order does not). Silica (Spectrosil synthetic fused silica) index is not printed; database SiO2 is used. A check with n = 1.460 at 546 nm reproduces the uncoated rhomb column a (84.55 deg for two reflections at 54 deg). Table III wavelengths 546, 405, 334, 289, 248, 214 nm. Recomputation of this stack at 546 nm with n_silica = 1.46 gives 43.09 deg per surface (Table III: 86.2/2).\nLayers from Table III footnote b (p. 1970); text p. 1971; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours total retardation 86.2, 88.1, 89.7, 90.7, 91.3, 90.5 deg at 546, 405, 334, 289, 248, 214 nm; paper 86.2, 87.7, 90.1, 90.8, 90.4, 90.5 deg (Table III). Exact at 546 and 214 nm, the only wavelengths with printed indices; the MgF2 and NdF3 dispersion between them is a two-term Cauchy fit, which is where the 0.4-0.9 deg differences come from.",
